+// surface's facing against the pixel's own geometric normal: 0 the same way (cosine above 0.7, as the ceiling or a
+// lamp's lower face seen from the ceiling), 1 across (a wall, a lamp's side), 2 opposite (cosine below -0.7, as the
+// floor); bit 4 the ray meets the hit surface from behind; bit 5 the hit pixel's material glows (its raw emission above
+// 0); bit 6 the hit pixel's entity holds a light; bits 8 to 15 the hit pixel's cell (below). The hit point is the pixel's own ray start plus the record's direction
+// times its hit distance, so for a block's ray, which starts at the block's chosen pixel, it lies up to a pixel off.
+ivec2 probeHitPixelOf(TracingPixel p, RayRecord record, WeighedRay ray)
+{
+    if (!ray.brings || record.light.a <= 0.0)
+        return ivec2(-1);
+    vec3 clip = clipOf(p.origin + ray.direction * record.light.a);
+    if (clip.z <= 0.0)
+        return ivec2(-1);
+    return clamp(ivec2(floor((0.5 * clip.xy / clip.z + 0.5) * frame.viewport.xy)), ivec2(0), ivec2(frame.viewport.xy) - 1);
